Here's a C# console application snippet that reads a text file line by line and counts the total number of words:

```csharp
using System;
using System.IO;

class Program
{
    static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Enter the path to the text file:");
        string filePath = Console.ReadLine();

        if (!File.Exists(filePath))
        {
            Console.WriteLine("File not found!");
            return;
        }

        int wordCount = 0;

        try
        {
            using (StreamReader reader = new StreamReader(filePath))
            {
                string line;
                while ((line = reader.ReadLine()) != null)
                {
                    // Split the line into words (using whitespace as delimiter)
                    string[] words = line.Split(new[] {' ', '\t', '\n', '\r'}, StringSplitOptions.RemoveEmptyEntries);
                    wordCount += words.Length;
                }
            }

            Console.WriteLine($"Total word count: {wordCount}");
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            Console.WriteLine($"An error occurred: {ex.Message}");
        }
    }
}
```

### Explanation:
1. The program prompts the user to enter a file path.
2. It checks if the file exists.
3. It uses `StreamReader` to read the file line by line.
4. For each line, it splits the text into words using whitespace (spaces, tabs, newlines) as delimiters.
5. It counts all non-empty words and accumulates the total count.
6. Finally, it prints the total word count.

### Alternative Version (More Robust Word Splitting):
If you want to handle punctuation better (e.g., treat "word," as "word"), you can use `Regex`:

```csharp
using System;
using System.IO;
using System.Text.RegularExpressions;

class Program
{
    static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Enter the path to the text file:");
        string filePath = Console.ReadLine();

        if (!File.Exists(filePath))
        {
            Console.WriteLine("File not found!");
            return;
        }

        int wordCount = 0;
        string pattern = @"\w+['\w]*|\w"; // Matches words with apostrophes or standalone letters

        try
        {
            using (StreamReader reader = new StreamReader(filePath))
            {
                string line;
                while ((line = reader.ReadLine()) != null)
                {
                    MatchCollection matches = Regex.Matches(line, pattern);
                    wordCount += matches.Count;
                }
            }

            Console.WriteLine($"Total word count: {wordCount}");
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            Console.WriteLine($"An error occurred: {ex.Message}");
        }
    }
}
```

This version uses regex to better handle punctuation and contractions. Choose the version that best fits your needs!
